fixes.fix:
PRIMARY, CSS only, no reinstall needed. Make the document at least screen-tall in standalone mode so WebKit hands the page the full viewport. Add to src/styles.css, ideally also to the inline <style> in index.html so it applies before the bundle CSS loads:
@media (display-mode: standalone) { html { min-height: min(100lvh, calc(100% + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px))); } }
This is the rule frappe-ui shipped in PR #1289 (merged 2026-10-13), and gameplan#608, Safe-Sips#17, hekate#41 and ferretry#668 adopted the same rule. In the iOS 26.2 simulator, innerHeight went from 812 to 874 and the fixed bar was drawn fully. Here it should take innerHeight from 873 to 932. Then #root (fixed, inset:0), .viewport and root.clientHeight follow, measure() gets H=932, and fit becomes 'normal'. The rule is stable. Before expansion it gives min(932, 873+59) = 932. After expansion 100% = 932, so it caps at 100lvh = 932 and there is no overflow or scroll. html min-height beats the existing height:100%, so nothing else in html/body needs to change. Keep html/body on a solid colour (already #04121a), because iOS paints outside-the-page areas with the solid colour and ignores gradients (hekate#41).

result.proposedFix:
Three changes. Use the screen height only in an iPhone home-screen app, size `.viewport` from the measurement, and measure from a probe that is not resized by the fix.

1) src/stage.tsx: add a size helper and use it in `measure()`
```ts
interface Box extends Layout {
  transform: string;
  /** the window the frame is drawn in, unrotated, in screen px; .viewport is sized to it */
  vw: number;
  vh: number;
}

/** The window the game draws in, in screen px. The probe is position:fixed; inset:0. */
function windowSize(probe: HTMLElement | null): [number, number] {
  const w = probe?.clientWidth || window.innerWidth;
  let h = probe?.clientHeight || window.innerHeight;
  // A Home Screen web app on an iPhone has no browser bars, so its window is the whole
  // screen. With the black-translucent status bar iOS can size the layout viewport as if
  // the status bar were opaque (screen height minus ~59pt) while still drawing from the
  // top of the screen. Trust the screen then, but only on a phone, only when the window
  // spans the screen's width (not iPad Split View / Slide Over / Stage Manager), and only
  // for a status-bar-sized shortfall (never an open keyboard).
  if ((navigator as Navigator & { standalone?: boolean }).standalone === true) {
    const s = Math.min(screen.width, screen.height); // iOS reports portrait values; min/max is safe either way
    const l = Math.max(screen.width, screen.height);
    const [sw, sh] = w <= h ? [s, l] : [l, s];
    if (s <= 600 && Math.abs(w - sw) <= 1 && h < sh && sh - h <= 80) h = sh;
  }
  return [w, h];
}

function measure(probe: HTMLElement | null): Box {
  const [VW, VH] = windowSize(probe);
  const [vt, vr, vb, vl] = readInsets(probe);
  // ... everything else unchanged ...
  return { k, h, sat, sab, content, fit: framed ? fitFor(h) : fitFor(content), framed, rot, transform, vw: VW, vh: VH };
}
```
- `navigator.standalone` exists only in iOS WebKit, so this never runs in Safari tabs (where it is `false`), in Android Chrome or Android installed apps (where `screen` includes the system bars), in in-app browsers, or in desktop browsers.
- In landscape the shortfall is 0, so the sideways-phone path keeps its exact numbers and the rotate/translate transform does not change.
- When iOS reports the correct size, the shortfall is 0 and this does nothing.

2) src/stage.tsx: Stage component
- Delete `const root = rootRef.current!;` (`noUnusedLocals` is on). Call `measure(probeRef.current)`.
- Add vw/vh to the `setBox` equality check: `... && old.vw === next.vw && old.vh === next.vh ? old : next`.
- Observe the probe, not `.viewport`: `ro.observe(probeRef.current!)`. `.viewport` will now have a fixed inline size, so watching it would miss rotations and resizes.
- Also add `window.addEventListener('pageshow', update)` and `document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', update)`, with matching removals. iOS can change the size while the web app is in the background.
- Size the container to exactly the frame's on-screen box: `<div className={...} ref={rootRef} style={box ? { width: box.vw, height: box.vh } : undefined}>`. Before the first measurement, `inset:0` still applies.
- In the non-rotated case the frame is DESIGN_W*k = VW wide and h*k = VH tall. Rotated, it covers VW x VH. So `.viewport`'s `overflow:hidden` never cuts anything off.
- `.viewport` (932) is allowed to be taller than the fixed `#root` (873). `#root` has no overflow clip, and fixed elements don't add scrollable area to the document, so the page still can't scroll.

3) src/styles.css `.inset-probe`: make it a full-viewport fixed probe that gives both the size and the insets
```css
.inset-probe {
  position: fixed;
  inset: 0;               /* was top:0; left:0; width:0; height:0 */
  box-sizing: border-box; /* clientWidth/Height = layout viewport; padding = safe-area insets */
  visibility: hidden;
  pointer-events: none;
  padding: env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px) env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px) env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px);
}
```
- The probe is a fixed child of a non-transformed `.viewport`, so it ignores `.viewport`'s size and clip.
- Its content box shrinks when the insets change, so the ResizeObserver also fires when safe-area values arrive late. Today it misses those.
- `readInsets()` works unchanged, and so does the `__psInsets` hook.
- `.viewport` can stay `inset:0`. The inline width/height take priority over bottom/right. Optionally write it as `left:0; top:0; width:100%; height:100%`.

Result on the 15 Pro Max home-screen app:
- VH becomes 932, `.viewport` and the frame are both 932pt, and the tab bar reaches the screen bottom.
- Content is 761 (`fit-normal`), so the logo goes back to the two-row layout.
- The splash lines up with the launch image.

Regression test: add a profile to `devices.js`:
- viewport 430x873, `screen:{width:430,height:932}` in `newContext`
- `addInitScript(() => Object.defineProperty(navigator, 'standalone', {get: () => true}))`
- insets `[59,0,34,0]`
- Check that `.viewport` and the stage's bounding box are 932 tall, that the stage has `fit-normal`, and that a tab button's bounding box reaches y > 873.
- Run the same profile with `standalone` false and check that it stays at 873. That is Safari-tab behaviour, unchanged.
- Chromium won't draw below 873, so check sizes only.

Optional device diagnostic to confirm the cause: a `?vp` flag that shows `probe.clientHeight`, `innerHeight`, `visualViewport.height/offsetTop`, `screen.height`, `documentElement.clientHeight`, `navigator.standalone` and the insets.

fixes.fix:
Primary, CSS (the working tree already has this in src/styles.css and the inline style in index.html): a page-height floor in standalone. Code:
@media (display-mode: standalone) { html { min-height: min(100lvh, calc(100% + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px))); } }
The manifest uses display: standalone, so the media query matches. Every reported working fix used this rule or html, body {min-height:100lvh}: frappe-ui #1289, raven #66, LiftLog #8, ferretry #668, hekate #41, home-account #494.

Drop overflow:hidden on body in standalone (or everywhere). Clipping is already done by #root {position:fixed; inset:0} and .viewport {overflow:hidden}. Keep overscroll-behavior:none. Code:
@media (display-mode: standalone) { body { overflow: visible; } }
Right now body { overflow: hidden } propagates to the viewport and makes the root scroller non-scrollable. None of the fixes reported as working had overflow:hidden on html/body, and LiftLog #8 explicitly removed `html, body { overflow: hidden }` in the same change that added the floor. Recovery by dragging, and probably the scrollTo(0,1) 'heal' in the WIP stage.tsx, need a scrollable root.

JS detection: keep laying out from the fixed root's clientHeight, which is the painted height. Detect the short state with the minimum of all the readings, not innerHeight alone, because iOS 27 was seen with innerHeight full but documentElement.clientHeight short. Code:
const tall = Math.max(screen.width, screen.height);
const got = Math.min(innerHeight, document.documentElement.clientHeight, root.clientHeight);
const gap = tall - got;
const short = navigator.standalone === true && innerWidth < innerHeight && gap >= 20 && Math.abs(gap - topInset) <= 4;
Re-measure on resize, visualViewport resize, pageshow, visibilitychange (visible), focusout and orientationchange, each with staggered retries (about 0/150/400/1000 ms).

Heal after the keyboard closes (iOS 17/18 shrink to 873). Toggle display on a full-viewport-height element with a forced reflow, after the input blurs and about 140 ms has passed, only if the height is more than 4 px below the largest seen. Code:
const r = document.getElementById('root')!; r.style.display = 'none'; void r.offsetHeight; r.style.display = '';
Save and restore scrollTop on inner scrollers around the toggle. dev.to/cederhook found this worked where scrollTo, position:fixed shell locking, interactive-widget=resizes-visual and height:100% did not.

result.betterFix:
The reviewer's tile shrink alone is not enough. Going from 38px to 30px saves about 24px, but Safari-tight phones are about 50px short and compact is about 55px short. Make two changes, both football only, so basketball does not change:

1. In Reveal, when `NFL && unbeaten`, leave out the two lines the banner makes redundant: the 'Final record' `.gameno` eyebrow and the 'Made the playoffs' `.qualify` line. A 17–0 team obviously made the playoffs, and the banner already states the record. This saves about 47px on tight and 43px on compact. An alternative is to show the banner where `.gameno` sits instead of inside `.verdict`.

2. Shrink the schedule on short frames:
```css
.fit-tight .sched span { height: 32px; }
.fit-compact .sched { margin-top: 10px; gap: 4px; }
.fit-compact .sched span { height: 28px; }
.fit-compact .sched b { font-size: 10px; }
```
This saves about 18px on both.

Together that is about 65px on tight and about 60px on compact, which fits 654px and 570px of content with the button's 12px bottom padding kept.

Then re-run devices.js with UNBEATEN=1 and `?sport=nfl` on se-safari, se-app, mini-safari, i15-safari and pm-safari. Confirm no 'control below the frame' or 'content overflows .reveal' reports. Also run once without UNBEATEN to confirm a normal NFL season still fits.
